  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThis afternoon into Tuesday Morning: Dry Conditions\n\nVisible satellite early this afternoon shows low level clouds \nremaining across much of the region. The low clouds should slowly \nclear out today and temperatures should rise into the upper 60s to \nmid 70s this afternoon. Some patchy fog may develop once again \ntonight over portions of the Upper Midwest, but it is not expected \nto be as widespread or as dense as it was this morning. Some \nisolated locations could still see visibility drop down to a mile or \nless though. \n\nTuesday Afternoon through Thursday: Widespread Rainfall\n\nAn upper level low pressure system will begin to become cutoff over \nthe Desert Southwest on Tuesday into Wednesday. Out ahead of the \nlow, moisture will surge northward towards the Southern Plains and \nthen eventually the Northern Plains to Great Lakes region. The \nincreased moisture will even be aided by Hurricane Polo after it \nmoves northeast out of Baja California into far northern Mexico. The \ncombination of synoptic scale forcing and a rich moisture \nenvironment will lead to widespread heavy rain from the Desert \nSouthwest, to the Plains, and portions of the Upper Midwest. \nWidespread totals of 2+ inches are possible throughout this entire \narea given this set-up.\n\nAs for our area, rain is expected to begin across western portions \nof the local forecast area on Tuesday afternoon. This initial round \nof rain will occur at the same time as PWAT values begin to increase \nupwards of 1.5-1.75 inches. MUCAPE values may only be as high as a \nfew hundred joules throughout the entire time period, so moderate to \nheavy rain may occur at times aided by convective processes. It \nstill appears the best potential for heavy rainfall that may lead to \nflooding concerns will be from Wednesday afternoon into the night. \nThis will coincide with the stronger push of moisture and it will\noccur out ahead of a southeastward moving cold front. Model \nguidance continues to vary on where the highest rain totals may \nfall, ranging from just to the northwest of the FA or across the\nsouthern half of the FA. Based on the synoptic set-up, we are \nleaning towards the southern solutions panning out which would \nbring in heavier rainfall totals to northeastern IA into far \nsouthwestern WI. Most likely rainfall totals continue to be in \nthe 1-3 inch range, with the higher totals (2.5-3 inches) across\nnortheastern IA. For some isolated locations, higher totals of \n3.5-4+ inches cannot be ruled out. These rainfall totals would \nlead to flooding concerns, especially for rivers and locations \nthat had just received a lot of rainfall in the last few weeks. \nThe rain potential will end from northwest to southeast on \nThursday as the cold front moves through and the mid level \nmoisture becomes displaced to the east/southeast of the region.\n\nFriday into the Weekend: Drier and Trending Cooler\n\nIn the wake of the cold frontal passage, temperatures should slowly \ntrend cooler this upcoming weekend as broad scale troughing attempts \nto dig south over the Great Lakes region. Daytime highs are not \nforecast to be all that much cooler, with temperatures remaining in \nthe mid 60s. Where the cooler air will be more noticeable will \nbe overnight, with overnight lows drop into the lower 40s to mid\n30s. Dry conditions are currently expected but there may be a \nlow chance for light rain on Saturday if moisture moves in quick\nenough out ahead of another southern surge in the upper level \ntrough.",
